### Concept 4: **NutriSense-SHC** — Virtual Soil-Sensor & Split-Dosage Prescription Engine
1. **Project Identity:**
   * **One-Line Description:** Spectral-soil fusion system that updates stale 3-year Soil Health Card records using Sentinel-2 Red-Edge (`NDRE`) chlorophyll tracking to prescribe variable split-fertilizer doses.
   * **Main Objective:** Fix India's **10.9:4.1:1 N:P:K imbalance** ([PMF IAS](https://www.pmfias.com/fertilizer-overuse-drivers-implications-challenges/)) and boost Nitrogen NUE above 30–45% ([PIB 2026](https://www.pib.gov.in/PressReleasePage.aspx?PRID=2237709)).
   * **Primary Target User:** Cereal, cotton, and maize farmers relying on generic fertilizer shop advice.
2. **Real-World Problem:** 250M+ Soil Health Cards exist ([ResearchGate 2026](https://www.researchgate.net/publication/404936058_Global_Agri_Vision_Artificial_Intelligence_Transforming_Agronomy_with_Predictive_Analytics)), but they are static village snapshots that do not track mid-season plant nitrogen uptake.
3. **Proposed Solution:** Uses Sentinel-2 Bands 5, 6, 7 (`NDRE` / Canopy Chlorophyll Content Index) as a "virtual leaf color chart" combined with SHC baseline N-P-K and SOC to calculate exact top-dressing urea/DAP needs.
4. **Innovation:** Turns free 20m Sentinel-2 Red-Edge bands into a dynamic in-season update layer on top of static government Soil Health Cards.
5. **AI Involvement:** CatBoost regression mapping `SHC Baseline + NDRE Slope + Cumulative Rain` to yield response per kg of added N.
6. **Data Requirements:** SHC district/village aggregates, ISRIC SoilGrids, Sentinel-2 L2A Red-Edge bands, ICAR fertilizer response curves.
7. **Technical Feasibility (15 Days):** **High.**
8. **User Benefits:** Cuts unnecessary 3rd urea top-dressing by 20–30% when canopy chlorophyll is already saturated (saving ₹600–₹1,200/acre).
9. **Limitations:** Cannot directly measure soil P or K from satellite reflectance; only infers canopy chlorophyll (N proxy).
10. **Demonstration Potential:** Plot heatmap showing nitrogen-saturated zones vs. nitrogen-deficient zones on a 5-acre farm.

